^KnowledgeCards_+KnowledgeCards_g5tR9r1gxCXjC0elZtftDIh3yoq1_*com.apple.UIKit.UISplitViewController.Root_+hasPendingSync_g5tR9r1gxCXjC0elZtftDIh3yoq1XWordBank_-didMigrateLegacy_g5tR9r1gxCXjC0elZtftDIh3yoq1_%WordBank_g5tR9r1gxCXjC0elZtftDIh3yoq1_didMigrateOldDefaults_-sample_sentences_g5tR9r1gxCXjC0elZtftDIh3yoq1Oj�[{"id":"F578B33A-5A5B-4CE9-B0D0-AC63FC0F34DE","text":"Japanese Pitch Accent\n\n•  High-High (平坦型 heitan-gata):  Pitch remains high throughout the word.\n    * Example:  neko (cat) -  High-High\n\n•  High-Low (頭高型 atama-takagata): Pitch starts high and falls low.\n    * Example:  oki (wake up) - High-Low\n\n•  Low-High (頭低型 atama-hikugata): Pitch starts low and rises high.\n    * Example:  hashi (bridge) - Low-High\n\n•  Low-Low (平板型 heiban-gata): Pitch remains low throughout the word.\n    * Example:  yama (mountain) - Low-Low"},{"id":"CC6C0905-4EAD-4A39-891A-DFE50448E8A4","text":"Time Expressions: に vs. は\n\n• に marks the point in time when an action occurs.\n\n  * Example:  8時に夕食を食べます。(8-ji ni yūshoku o tabemasu.) - I eat dinner at 8 o'clock.  (Focus: at what time the action happens)\n\n\n• は marks the topic of the sentence.  When used with time, it introduces the time as the topic of discussion. The time itself isn't necessarily directly related to the action's occurrence.\n\n  * Example:  8時は夕食の時間です。(8-ji wa yūshoku no jikan desu.) - 8 o'clock is dinner time. (Focus: 8 o'clock is being described)\n\n  * Example: 今朝は雨が降った。(Kesa wa ame ga futta.) - This morning it rained. (Focus: This morning is the topic; the rain occurred then).  (Note:  the rain didn't necessarily *start* at the very beginning of the morning).\n\n•  In short: に focuses on *when* something happens, while は sets the *time frame* for the discussion."},{"id":"CD0B4DC7-7BB0-4CDB-B8A6-147CF7D86D60","text":"Connecting Adjectives to Nouns\n\n*Directly Attaching the I-Adjective\n\n•  The i-adjective changes its ending depending on the noun.\n    *  For example:  赤いリンゴ (akai ringo - red apple).  The i-adjective 赤い (akai - red) directly modifies the noun リンゴ (ringo - apple).\n\n\n*Directly Attaching the Na-Adjective\n\n•  The na-adjective is followed by な (na) before the noun.\n    *  For example:  静かな森 (shizukana mori - quiet forest).  The na-adjective 静か (shizuka - quiet) needs な (na) to connect to the noun 森 (mori - forest).\n\n\n*Using the Noun as a Modifier\n\n•   Some nouns can directly modify other nouns. The modifying noun usually comes before the modified noun.\n    *  For example:  学生時代 (gakusei jidai - student times\/student days).  学生 (gakusei - student) modifies 時代 (jidai - times\/era).\n\n*Using a Particle (の - no)\n\n•  The particle の (no) indicates possession or attribution.\n    *  For example:  友達の家 (tomodachi no ie - friend's house).  友達 (tomodachi - friend) modifies 家 (ie - house) through possessive の (no)."},{"id":"3C6A465F-0988-4398-99DF-091755F8EACD","text":"Topic: が vs. は\n\n• Subject Marker (が)\n\n  * Marks the grammatical subject of a sentence, specifically introducing new information or the topic being focused on for the first time.\n  * Indicates the subject performing an action or being described.\n  * Often translates to \"as for,\" \"it is,\" or is omitted in English.\n  * Example: 猫が寝ている。(Neko ga neteiru.) - The cat is sleeping. (Focus is on the cat's sleeping)\n\n\n• Topic Marker (は)\n\n  * Marks the topic of a sentence, something already known or established in the conversation.\n  * Indicates the subject about which something is being said.\n  * Often translates to \"as for,\" \"regarding,\" or is omitted in English.\n  * Example: 猫はかわいい。(Neko wa kawaii.) - As for the cat, it is cute. (The cat is already known; the focus is on its cuteness)\n\n\n• Key Difference\n\n  * が introduces new information; は presents information about a known topic.\n  * A sentence can have only one が, but multiple は are possible.\n\n\n• Example illustrating the difference:\n\n  * 猫が走った。(Neko ga hashitta.) - A cat ran. (Introducing a cat running for the first time)\n  * 猫は走った。(Neko wa hashitta.) - The cat ran. (Referring to a cat already mentioned, focusing on its action)\n"},{"id":"026464F2-3CD2-4531-B3D2-3E31C7F97E65","text":"Using なな (nana) and しち (shichi) for Seven\n\n• Number vs. Counter\n\n  * なな (nana) is used when seven is a standalone number.\n     * Example: 七 (nana) is seven.  七匹 (nana hiki) is seven (animals).\n  * しち (shichi) is used when seven is part of a counter word.\n     * Example: 七月 (shichi-gatsu) is July (month counter).  七十 (shichi-juu) is seventy (ten counter)."},{"id":"667FE5D0-6933-42F2-8A44-9E11662B2389","text":"ぐらい\/くらい and ごろ\/頃\n\n• Meaning\n\n  * ぐらい\/くらい: approximately, around, about (quantity, degree)\n  * ごろ\/頃: around, about (time)\n\n• Usage\n\n  * ぐらい\/くらい: Used with numbers, amounts, and degrees.  Examples:\n    * 三時間ぐらい勉強しました。(San-jikan gurai benkyou shimashita.) - I studied for about three hours.\n    * 百円ぐらい。(Hyaku-en gurai) - About 100 yen.\n    * ちょっと疲れたぐらい。(Chotto tsukareta gurai) - I'm a little tired.\n\n  * ごろ\/頃: Used to indicate approximate time. Examples:\n    * 十時頃。(Juuji goro) - Around 10 o'clock.\n    * 晩ご飯の頃。(Bangohan no goro) - Around dinner time.\n    * 春の頃。(Haru no goro) - Around springtime.\n\n• Key Difference\n\n  * The core difference lies in what they modify: ぐらい\/くらい modifies quantities or degrees, while ごろ\/頃 modifies time.  While both express approximation, their usage contexts are distinct."},{"id":"5ADB38AB-D0EF-42D4-A2FC-4A61001039F6","text":"あります vs. います: Existence\n\n•   あります (arimasu) indicates the existence of inanimate objects, plants, places, and abstract concepts.\n\n    *   Example:\n        *   机の上に本があります。(Tsukue no ue ni hon ga arimasu.)\n        *   There is a book on the desk.\n\n•   います (imasu) indicates the existence of animate objects (living things), specifically people and animals.\n\n    *   Example:\n        *   部屋に猫がいます。(Heya ni neko ga imasu.)\n        *   There is a cat in the room.\n\n•   Which one to use depends on WHAT exists, not WHERE it exists.\n\n    *   Example:\n        *   公園に木があります。(Kouen ni ki ga arimasu.)\n        *   There is a tree in the park. (Tree = plant = あります)\n        *   公園に子供がいます。(Kouen ni kodomo ga imasu.)\n        *   There are children in the park. (Children = people = います)"},{"id":"6C3762F6-A229-4F31-B4B6-46B8B540370F","text":"### Expressing Wants: ~たいです\n\n•   The ~たいです form expresses a desire to do something. It attaches to the stem of a verb (the part before -ます).\n\n•   ます is dropped from the verb.\n\n•   みます (mimasu - to see) becomes み (mi). Then たいです (tai desu) is added.\n\n•   みます -> み + たいです = みたいです (mitai desu - want to see)\n\n•   The particle changes from を (o) to が (ga). This is because the verb changes into an adjective. The \"object\" of your desire becomes the \"subject\" of your desire.\n\n•   Examples:\n\n    *   映画を見ます。(eiga o mimasu - I watch a movie.) -> 映画が見たいです。(eiga ga mitai desu - I want to watch a movie.)\n    *   寿司を食べます。(sushi o tabemasu - I eat sushi.) -> 寿司が食べたいです。(sushi ga tabetai desu - I want to eat sushi.)\n    *   日本へ行きます。(Nihon e ikimasu - I go to Japan.) -> 日本へ行きたいです。(Nihon e ikitai desu - I want to go to Japan.) (*Note*: The particle へ (e) does not change.)"},{"id":"C7C8E2B8-97B8-4BB6-9190-6AE9ADBE7D4F","text":"Counting in Japanese: Different Counters\n\n•   General Idea\n\n    Japanese often uses specific counters after the number when counting things. These counters depend on what you are counting (people, objects, animals, etc.).\n    Think of it like saying \"a slice of\" pizza vs. \"a loaf of\" bread in English.\n\n•   Counter: ～り (ri) - For People\n\n    This is used when counting people.\n\n    *   1人 (ひとり - hitori): One person\n    *   2人 (ふたり - futari): Two people\n    *   3人 (さんにん - san nin): Three people\n    *   4人 (よにん - yo nin): Four people\n    *   5人 (ごにん - go nin): Five people\n    *   6人 (ろくにん - roku nin): Six people\n    *   7人 (しちにん \/ ななにん - shichi nin \/ nana nin): Seven people\n    *   8人 (はちにん - hachi nin): Eight people\n    *   9人 (きゅうにん - kyuu nin): Nine people\n\n•   Counter: ～つ (tsu) - For General Objects (up to 10)\n\n    This is an older, more traditional counter for general items. Commonly used for counting up to 10.\n\n    *   1つ (ひとつ - hitotsu): One object\n    *   2つ (ふたつ - futatsu): Two objects\n    *   3つ (みっつ - mittsu): Three objects\n    *   4つ (よっつ - yottsu): Four objects\n    *   5つ (いつつ - itsutsu): Five objects\n    *   6つ (むっつ - muttsu): Six objects\n    *   7つ (ななつ - nanatsu): Seven objects\n    *   8つ (やっつ - yattsu): Eight objects\n    *   9つ (ここのつ - kokonotsu): Nine objects\n\n•   Counter: ～匹 (hiki) - For Small to Medium Animals\n\n    This is used for counting small to medium-sized animals like cats, dogs, fish, and insects.\n\n    *   1匹 (いっぴき - ippiki): One animal\n    *   2匹 (にひき - nihiki): Two animals\n    *   3匹 (さんびき - sanbiki): Three animals\n    *   4匹 (よんひき - yonhiki): Four animals\n    *   5匹 (ごひき - gohiki): Five animals\n    *   6匹 (ろっぴき - roppiki): Six animals\n    *   7匹 (ななひき - nanahiki): Seven animals\n    *   8匹 (はっぴき - happiki): Eight animals\n    *   9匹 (きゅうひき - kyuuhiki): Nine animals\n\n    Note: The pronunciation of the number + counter can change (like ippiki and roppiki). This is called sound change or phonetic change."},{"id":"20B87456-8137-4E8C-B139-9BD5A7394F4A","text":"Months in Japanese (Hiragana)\n\n•   Basic Structure: Number + \"gatsu\" (がつ)\n\n•   Write the number in hiragana, then add \"gatsu\".\n\n•   Exceptions: Some months have irregular readings.\n\n•   Counting:\n\n    *   January: いちがつ (ichigatsu)\n    *   February: にがつ (nigatsu)\n    *   March: さんがつ (sangatsu)\n    *   April: しがつ (shigatsu) - Note: \"shi\" is used instead of \"yon\"\n    *   May: ごがつ (gogatsu)\n    *   June: ろくがつ (rokugatsu)\n    *   July: しちがつ (shichigatsu) - Note: \"shichi\" is used instead of \"nana\"\n    *   August: はちがつ (hachigatsu)\n    *   September: くがつ (kugatsu) - Note: \"ku\" is used instead of \"kyuu\"\n    *   October: じゅうがつ (juugatsu)\n    *   November: じゅういちがつ (juuichigatsu)\n    *   December: じゅうにがつ (juunigatsu)"},{"id":"D878BFAD-8FCA-4F88-9FA1-09A41AC4BA54","text":"## い-Adjective Negation\n\n•   Replace the い at the end of the adjective with くない (kunai).\n\n    *   Example: 忙しい (isogashii - busy) -> 忙しくない (isogashikunai - not busy)\n    *   Example: 楽しい (tanoshii - fun) -> 楽しくない (tanoshikunai - not fun)\n\n•   You can then add です (desu) to make it polite.\n\n    *   忙しくないです (isogashikunai desu - I am not busy.)\n    *   楽しくないです (tanoshikunai desu - It is not fun.)"},{"id":"5034FCF3-9B78-4979-A81A-0B49C8F4DEED","text":"Counting Days in Japanese\n\n• Days 1-10 and the 20th have special readings. Memorize these!\n\n    * 1st: tsuitachi (ついたち)\n    * 2nd: futsuka (ふつか)\n    * 3rd: mikka (みっか)\n    * 4th: yokka (よっか)\n    * 5th: itsuka (いつか)\n    * 6th: muika (むいか)\n    * 7th: nanoka (なのか)\n    * 8th: yooka (ようか)\n    * 9th: kokonoka (ここのか)\n    * 10th: tooka (とおか)\n    * 20th: hatsuka (はつか)\n\n• For days 11-19 and 21-31, combine the number + \"nichi\" (にち).\n\n    * 11th: juuichinichi (じゅういちにち)\n    * 14th: juuyokkanichi is incorrect. Instead use juuyokka (じゅうよっか)\n    * 15th: juugonichi (じゅうごにち)\n    * 23rd: nijuusannichi (にじゅうさんにち)\n    * 31st: sanjuuichinichi (さんじゅういちにち)\n\n• Asking \"What day of the month is it?\"\n\n    *  何日ですか (nan nichi desu ka?)"},{"id":"E249267F-CCC1-4DA2-8C2F-452EFDD68FEF","text":"## Referring to Family Members: Uchi vs. Soto\n\nThe way you refer to family members changes depending on whether you are speaking within your family (uchi\/内) or to someone outside your family (soto\/外). This reflects Japanese modesty and respect. When speaking to someone outside your family, you refer to your own family members with more humble terms.\n\n### Grandparents\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Grandfather: おじいさん (Ojiisan)\n    *   Grandmother: おばあさん (Obaasan)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Grandfather: 祖父 (Sofu)\n    *   Grandmother: 祖母 (Sobo)\n\n### Parents\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Father: お父さん (Otousan)\n    *   Mother: お母さん (Okaasan)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Father: 父 (Chichi)\n    *   Mother: 母 (Haha)\n\n    *Example:* \"My father works at a bank.\"\n    *Speaking to a friend:* 父は銀行で働いています。(Chichi wa ginkou de hataraiteimasu.)\n\n### Siblings\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Older Brother: お兄さん (Oniisan)\n    *   Older Sister: お姉さん (Oneesan)\n    *   Younger Brother: 弟 (Otouto)\n    *   Younger Sister: 妹 (Imouto)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Older Brother: 兄 (Ani)\n    *   Older Sister: 姉 (Ane)\n    *   Younger Brother: 弟 (Otouto)\n    *   Younger Sister: 妹 (Imouto)\n\n    *Example:* \"My older brother is a doctor.\"\n    *Speaking to a colleague:* 兄は医者です。(Ani wa isha desu.)\n\n    *Note:* For younger siblings, the term is the same both inside and outside the family.\n\n### Spouse\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Husband: あなた (Anata), 旦那さん (Danna-san) (less common when directly addressing)\n    *   Wife: あなた (Anata), 奥さん (Okusan) (less common when directly addressing)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Husband: 夫 (Otto), 主人 (Shujin)\n    *   Wife: 妻 (Tsuma), 家内 (Kanai)\n\n    *Example:* \"My wife likes to read books.\"\n    *Speaking to a neighbor:* 妻は本を読むのが好きです。(Tsuma wa hon o yomu no ga suki desu.)\n\n### Children\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Child: (Child's name) + ちゃん (chan) or くん (kun)\n    *   Son: (Son's name) + くん (kun)\n    *   Daughter: (Daughter's name) + ちゃん (chan)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Son: 息子 (Musuko)\n    *   Daughter: 娘 (Musume)\n\n    *Example:* \"My daughter is 5 years old.\"\n    *Speaking to someone at the park:* 娘は5歳です。(Musume wa go-sai desu.)\n\n### Katakana Note\n\nKatakana is generally not used for family member terms except when emphasizing or writing foreign names.\n\n### Grammar Note\n\nWhen talking TO your family members, you always use お父さん etc. When talking ABOUT your family to someone OUTSIDE your family, you use 父 etc.\n\n### Importance\n\nUsing the correct terms shows respect and understanding of Japanese culture. It's an important distinction to make when communicating effectively."},{"id":"73CEFBAA-0E91-48F7-8542-A1B46D1437F9","text":"Particle に and へ for Direction\n\nBoth に and へ can be used to indicate direction or destination when paired with verbs of motion like 行きます (ikimasu - to go), 来ます (kimasu - to come), 帰ります (kaerimasu - to return), etc.\n\nWhen to Use に\n\nUse に when you want to emphasize the specific destination or endpoint of the movement. It highlights the place where the action will be completed.\n\nExamples:\n•   学校に行きます。 (Gakkou ni ikimasu.) - I am going to school. (Focus on school as the destination.)\n•   日本に行きます。 (Nihon ni ikimasu.) - I am going to Japan. (Focus on Japan as the destination.)\n•   家にかえります。 (Ie ni kaerimasu.) - I am returning home. (Focus on home as the destination.)\n\nWhen to Use へ\n\nUse へ when you want to emphasize the direction or the general trajectory of the movement. It suggests moving towards a place rather than arriving at it as the sole focus.\n\nExamples:\n•   学校へ行きます。 (Gakkou e ikimasu.) - I am going towards school. (Focus on the direction of travel.)\n•   駅へ向かいます。 (Eki e mukaimasu.) - I am heading towards the station. (Emphasizes the direction of movement.)\n•   海へ行きたいです。 (Umi e ikitai desu.) - I want to go to the sea. (Implies a general direction.)\n\nInterchangeability\n\nIn many common situations, particularly with 行きます, に and へ are interchangeable and the nuance is very slight.\n\nExample:\n•   東京に行きます。 (Toukyou ni ikimasu.) - I am going to Tokyo.\n•   東京へ行きます。 (Toukyou e ikimasu.) - I am going towards Tokyo.\n\nBoth are perfectly natural and understandable. The choice often depends on personal preference or the specific nuance the speaker wishes to convey."},{"id":"45AD3586-1F3A-4194-8F53-BBF0F24D9196","text":"Grammar Note: 〜てはいけません\n\nThis grammar pattern expresses prohibition, meaning \"must not do\" or \"cannot do.\"\n\n•   Structure: Verb (te-form) + はいけません\n•   Meaning: It is not allowed to do [verb].\n•   Usage: This is a polite but firm way to tell someone they are not permitted to do something.\n\nExample 1:\n食べ物を持ってはいけません。\n(Tabemono o motte wa ikemasen.)\nYou must not bring food.\n\nExample 2:\nここでは話してはいけません。\n(Koko de wa hanashite wa ikemasen.)\nYou must not talk here.\n\nExample 3 (Your sentence):\nまだ家に帰ってはいけません。\n(Mada ie ni kaette wa ikemasen.)\nYou must not go home yet."},{"id":"203208A0-5D77-449C-B7C7-FDF8AEB2C3E3","text":"Adjective + する \n\nGrammar Breakdown: 先生はこの授業を面白くします (Sensei wa kono jugyou o omoshiroku shimasu)\n\nSubject-Object-Verb Structure\n\n•   **Subject:** 先生 (Sensei) - The teacher\n•   **Topic Particle:** は (wa) - Marks the topic of the sentence.\n•   **Object:** この授業 (kono jugyou) - This class\n•   **Object Particle:** を (o) - Marks the direct object.\n•   **Adjective + する (suru): Making Something [Adjective]**\n    *   The grammar point here is using the i-adjective form plus く (ku) followed by する (shimasu). This construction means \"to make something [adjective].\"\n    *   The base i-adjective is 面白い (omoshiroi) - interesting.\n    *   Changing 面白い to 面白く (omoshiroku) is the adverbial form, which is used before する.\n    *   So, 面白くします (omoshiroku shimasu) means \"makes [it] interesting.\"\n•   **Verb:** します (shimasu) - The polite form of する (suru), meaning \"to do\" or in this case, \"to make.\"\n\nIn summary, the sentence means \"The teacher makes this class interesting.\"\n\nExample:\n•   この本を面白く読みました。(Kono hon o omoshiroku yomimashita.) - I read this book in an interesting way. (Here, 面白く modifies the verb 読みました, meaning \"read interestingly\")\n•   先生はこの本を面白くしました。(Sensei wa kono hon o omoshiroku shimashita.) - The teacher made this book interesting. (Here, 面白く modifies the verb しました, meaning \"made interesting\")"},{"id":"2FC1B400-FECE-4829-B6D6-8E9661AD624F","text":"Grammar Note: Using 「多い」 with Nouns\n\nTopic: Describing the abundance of something.\n\nThe pattern: Noun + が + 多い + Adjective\n\nExplanation:\n•   「多い」 (ooi) is an adjective meaning \"many\" or \"a lot of\".\n•   When describing a noun that has *many* of something, we use the particle 「が」 to link the thing that is numerous to 「多い」.\n•   This construct then functions as an adjective modifying a main noun.\n\nExample:\n•   本が多い部屋 (hon ga ooi heya) - A room with many books.\n•   人が多い場所 (hito ga ooi basho) - A place with many people.\n\nApplying to your sentence:\n•   車が多い道 (kuruma ga ooi michi) - A road with many cars.\n•   Therefore, 車が多い道は危ないです (kuruma ga ooi michi wa abunai desu) means \"A road with many cars is dangerous.\""},{"id":"46A33837-DDCD-4EE9-9137-F27CE769160B","text":"Expressing preference\n\nHere is a breakdown of the sentence: スカートよりズボンのほうが好きです (Sukāto yori zubon no hō ga suki desu).\n\nGrammatical Breakdown:\n\n•   スカート (sukāto)\n    *   Noun (skirt)\n•   より (yori)\n    *   Particle indicating comparison. It means \"than\".\n    *   It marks the item being compared against.\n•   ズボン (zubon)\n    *   Noun (pants\/trousers)\n•   の (no)\n    *   Possessive particle. In this construction, it indicates possession or association.\n    *   Here, it links \"ズボン\" (pants) to \"ほうが\" (preferable).\n•   ほうが (hō ga)\n    *   This is a grammatical construction used to express preference.\n    *   It literally means \"the side of...\" or \"the way of...\". When used with a noun + の, it means \"more preferable\" or \"preferable\".\n    *   The structure is: Noun A + より + Noun B + のほうが + Adjective\/Verb. This means \"Noun B is preferred over Noun A\".\n•   好きです (suki desu)\n    *   Adjective + Copula (is liked). \"Suki\" is an adjective meaning \"likable\" or \"fond of\". \"Desu\" is the polite copula.\n    *   Together, it means \"like\".\n\nRelated Grammar: Expressing Preferences\n\n•   The patternNoun A + より + Noun B + のほうが + Adjective\/Verb is used to state a preference for Noun B over Noun A.\n    *   Example:\n        *   猫より犬のほうが好きです。(Neko yori inu no hō ga suki desu.)\n        *   I like dogs more than cats.\n•   You can also use verbs in this structure, although it's less common for simple likes\/dislikes. In that case, the verb would usually be in its plain form before the のほうが.\n    *   Example:\n        *   本を読むよりテレビを見るほうが好きです。(Hon o yomu yori terebi o miru hō ga suki desu.)\n        *   I like watching TV more than reading books."},{"id":"258C0151-0ACD-44E2-BA90-46C8B98C3B52","text":"Sentence: 今日は学校が休みだと思います\nTranslation: I think school is closed today.\n\n•   だと (da to)\n    *   だ (da): copula (plain form of です - desu). It links the subject\/topic to the predicate.\n    *   と (to): quotation particle. It introduces a clause or phrase that is being thought, said, or believed.\n    *   This combination forms a subordinate clause \"school is closed\".\n\nPutting it together:\n\nThe sentence is structured as: Topic + [Subject + Predicate of clause] + Verb of thinking.\n\n•   **[Clause being thought]:** 学校が休み (gakkou ga yasumi) - \"school is closed\"\n•   **[Verb of thinking]:** 思います (omoimasu) - \"I think\"\n•   **[Topic]:** 今日は (kyou wa) - \"As for today,\"\n\nThe full sentence means: As for today, I think that school is closed.\n\nRelated Idea: Expressing Belief or Opinion\n\n•   The pattern [Clause] + と思います ([Clause] + to omoimasu) is used to express what you think or believe.\n    *   Example: 明日は晴れると思います。(Ashita wa hareru to omoimasu.) - I think it will be sunny tomorrow.\n    *   Example: 彼は親切だと思います。(Kare wa shinsetsu da to omoimasu.) - I think he is kind. (Note: When the clause is a noun or na-adjective, you use だ before と)."},{"id":"5C7698EF-3B59-471D-956A-4043C502760A","text":"Grammar Note: The Role of の in \"私は歌うのが上手です\"\n\nFunction of の\n\nIn this sentence, の acts as a nominalizer. It turns a verb or verb phrase into a noun or noun phrase.\n\nStructure\n\nVerb (or Verb Phrase) + の + Topic Marker (は, が) + Adjective\/Noun + です\n\nExplanation\n\n1.  \"歌う\" (utau) is the verb \"to sing.\"\n2.  \"歌うの\" (utau no) transforms the act of singing into a noun phrase, meaning \"the act of singing\" or \"singing\" as an activity.\n3.  \"歌うのが\" (utau no ga) then marks this noun phrase \"singing\" as the subject of the adjective \"上手です\" (jōzu desu).\n\nExamples\n\n•   読むのが好きです (yomu no ga suki desu) - I like reading.\n    *   読む (yomu) - to read\n    *   読むの (yomu no) - reading (as an activity)\n    *   好きです (suki desu) - like\n•   走るのが速いです (hashiru no ga hayai desu) - I am fast at running.\n    *   走る (hashiru) - to run\n    *   走るの (hashiru no) - running (as an activity)\n    *   速いです (hayai desu) - is fast"},{"id":"08418F36-1743-4ED6-AEAC-AAAFE10DD6EE","text":"Understanding Verbs for \"To Work\"\n\nHere's a breakdown of the differences between 働きます, 勤めます, and 仕事します.\n\n働きます (hatarakimasu)\n\n•   This is the most general verb for \"to work\" or \"to be employed.\"\n•   It refers to the act of performing labor or carrying out a job.\n•   It can be used in many contexts, from formal employment to casual labor.\n\nExamples:\n•   会社で働きます。(Kaisha de hatarakimasu.) - I work at a company.\n•   毎日働かなければなりません。(Mainichi hatarakenakereba narimasen.) - I have to work every day.\n•   彼は一生懸命働いている。(Kare wa isshoukenmei hataraite iru.) - He is working hard.\n\n勤めます (tsutomesmasu)\n\n•   This verb specifically means \"to be employed by\" or \"to work for\" a company or organization.\n•   It implies a more formal and stable employment situation.\n•   It focuses on belonging to a specific employer.\n\nExamples:\n•   A社に勤めています。(A-sha ni tsutomete imasu.) - I am employed by Company A.\n•   大学に勤めていた。(Daigaku ni tsutomete ita.) - I was employed by the university.\n•   彼はその会社に長く勤めている。(Kare wa sono kaisha ni nagaku tsutomete iru.) - He has been working for that company for a long time.\n\n仕事します (shigoto shimasu)\n\n•   This is a compound verb meaning \"to do work\" or \"to have a job.\"\n•   It emphasizes the \"work\" (仕事) itself rather than the act of working in a general sense or being employed by someone.\n•   It can refer to a specific task, profession, or the general concept of having something to do.\n\nExamples:\n•   新しい仕事をします。(Atarashii shigoto o shimasu.) - I will do a new job\/task.\n•   私の仕事は医者です。(Watashi no shigoto wa isha desu.) - My job is a doctor. (This uses the noun form, but in speech, one might say 医者の仕事をします - Isha no shigoto o shimasu - I do the work of a doctor.)\n•   今日は忙しくて、たくさんの仕事をしました。(Kyou wa isogashikute, takusan no shigoto o shimashita.) - Today I was busy and did a lot of work."}]OP�[{"id":"F578B33A-5A5B-4CE9-B0D0-AC63FC0F34DE","text":"Japanese Pitch Accent\n\n•  High-High (平坦型 heitan-gata):  Pitch remains high throughout the word.\n    * Example:  neko (cat) -  High-High\n\n•  High-Low (頭高型 atama-takagata): Pitch starts high and falls low.\n    * Example:  oki (wake up) - High-Low\n\n•  Low-High (頭低型 atama-hikugata): Pitch starts low and rises high.\n    * Example:  hashi (bridge) - Low-High\n\n•  Low-Low (平板型 heiban-gata): Pitch remains low throughout the word.\n    * Example:  yama (mountain) - Low-Low"},{"id":"CC6C0905-4EAD-4A39-891A-DFE50448E8A4","text":"Time Expressions: に vs. は\n\n• に marks the point in time when an action occurs.\n\n  * Example:  8時に夕食を食べます。(8-ji ni yūshoku o tabemasu.) - I eat dinner at 8 o'clock.  (Focus: at what time the action happens)\n\n\n• は marks the topic of the sentence.  When used with time, it introduces the time as the topic of discussion. The time itself isn't necessarily directly related to the action's occurrence.\n\n  * Example:  8時は夕食の時間です。(8-ji wa yūshoku no jikan desu.) - 8 o'clock is dinner time. (Focus: 8 o'clock is being described)\n\n  * Example: 今朝は雨が降った。(Kesa wa ame ga futta.) - This morning it rained. (Focus: This morning is the topic; the rain occurred then).  (Note:  the rain didn't necessarily *start* at the very beginning of the morning).\n\n•  In short: に focuses on *when* something happens, while は sets the *time frame* for the discussion."},{"id":"026464F2-3CD2-4531-B3D2-3E31C7F97E65","text":"Using なな (nana) and しち (shichi) for Seven\n\n• Number vs. Counter\n\n  * なな (nana) is used when seven is a standalone number.\n     * Example: 七 (nana) is seven.  七匹 (nana hiki) is seven (animals).\n  * しち (shichi) is used when seven is part of a counter word.\n     * Example: 七月 (shichi-gatsu) is July (month counter).  七十 (shichi-juu) is seventy (ten counter)."},{"id":"667FE5D0-6933-42F2-8A44-9E11662B2389","text":"ぐらい\/くらい and ごろ\/頃\n\n• Meaning\n\n  * ぐらい\/くらい: approximately, around, about (quantity, degree)\n  * ごろ\/頃: around, about (time)\n\n• Usage\n\n  * ぐらい\/くらい: Used with numbers, amounts, and degrees.  Examples:\n    * 三時間ぐらい勉強しました。(San-jikan gurai benkyou shimashita.) - I studied for about three hours.\n    * 百円ぐらい。(Hyaku-en gurai) - About 100 yen.\n    * ちょっと疲れたぐらい。(Chotto tsukareta gurai) - I'm a little tired.\n\n  * ごろ\/頃: Used to indicate approximate time. Examples:\n    * 十時頃。(Juuji goro) - Around 10 o'clock.\n    * 晩ご飯の頃。(Bangohan no goro) - Around dinner time.\n    * 春の頃。(Haru no goro) - Around springtime.\n\n• Key Difference\n\n  * The core difference lies in what they modify: ぐらい\/くらい modifies quantities or degrees, while ごろ\/頃 modifies time.  While both express approximation, their usage contexts are distinct."},{"id":"C7C8E2B8-97B8-4BB6-9190-6AE9ADBE7D4F","text":"Counting in Japanese: Different Counters\n\n•   General Idea\n\n    Japanese often uses specific counters after the number when counting things. These counters depend on what you are counting (people, objects, animals, etc.).\n    Think of it like saying \"a slice of\" pizza vs. \"a loaf of\" bread in English.\n\n•   Counter: ～り (ri) - For People\n\n    This is used when counting people.\n\n    *   1人 (ひとり - hitori): One person\n    *   2人 (ふたり - futari): Two people\n    *   3人 (さんにん - san nin): Three people\n    *   4人 (よにん - yo nin): Four people\n    *   5人 (ごにん - go nin): Five people\n    *   6人 (ろくにん - roku nin): Six people\n    *   7人 (しちにん \/ ななにん - shichi nin \/ nana nin): Seven people\n    *   8人 (はちにん - hachi nin): Eight people\n    *   9人 (きゅうにん - kyuu nin): Nine people\n\n•   Counter: ～つ (tsu) - For General Objects (up to 10)\n\n    This is an older, more traditional counter for general items. Commonly used for counting up to 10.\n\n    *   1つ (ひとつ - hitotsu): One object\n    *   2つ (ふたつ - futatsu): Two objects\n    *   3つ (みっつ - mittsu): Three objects\n    *   4つ (よっつ - yottsu): Four objects\n    *   5つ (いつつ - itsutsu): Five objects\n    *   6つ (むっつ - muttsu): Six objects\n    *   7つ (ななつ - nanatsu): Seven objects\n    *   8つ (やっつ - yattsu): Eight objects\n    *   9つ (ここのつ - kokonotsu): Nine objects\n\n•   Counter: ～匹 (hiki) - For Small to Medium Animals\n\n    This is used for counting small to medium-sized animals like cats, dogs, fish, and insects.\n\n    *   1匹 (いっぴき - ippiki): One animal\n    *   2匹 (にひき - nihiki): Two animals\n    *   3匹 (さんびき - sanbiki): Three animals\n    *   4匹 (よんひき - yonhiki): Four animals\n    *   5匹 (ごひき - gohiki): Five animals\n    *   6匹 (ろっぴき - roppiki): Six animals\n    *   7匹 (ななひき - nanahiki): Seven animals\n    *   8匹 (はっぴき - happiki): Eight animals\n    *   9匹 (きゅうひき - kyuuhiki): Nine animals\n\n    Note: The pronunciation of the number + counter can change (like ippiki and roppiki). This is called sound change or phonetic change."},{"id":"20B87456-8137-4E8C-B139-9BD5A7394F4A","text":"Months in Japanese (Hiragana)\n\n•   Basic Structure: Number + \"gatsu\" (がつ)\n\n•   Write the number in hiragana, then add \"gatsu\".\n\n•   Exceptions: Some months have irregular readings.\n\n•   Counting:\n\n    *   January: いちがつ (ichigatsu)\n    *   February: にがつ (nigatsu)\n    *   March: さんがつ (sangatsu)\n    *   April: しがつ (shigatsu) - Note: \"shi\" is used instead of \"yon\"\n    *   May: ごがつ (gogatsu)\n    *   June: ろくがつ (rokugatsu)\n    *   July: しちがつ (shichigatsu) - Note: \"shichi\" is used instead of \"nana\"\n    *   August: はちがつ (hachigatsu)\n    *   September: くがつ (kugatsu) - Note: \"ku\" is used instead of \"kyuu\"\n    *   October: じゅうがつ (juugatsu)\n    *   November: じゅういちがつ (juuichigatsu)\n    *   December: じゅうにがつ (juunigatsu)"},{"id":"D878BFAD-8FCA-4F88-9FA1-09A41AC4BA54","text":"## い-Adjective Negation\n\n•   Replace the い at the end of the adjective with くない (kunai).\n\n    *   Example: 忙しい (isogashii - busy) -> 忙しくない (isogashikunai - not busy)\n    *   Example: 楽しい (tanoshii - fun) -> 楽しくない (tanoshikunai - not fun)\n\n•   You can then add です (desu) to make it polite.\n\n    *   忙しくないです (isogashikunai desu - I am not busy.)\n    *   楽しくないです (tanoshikunai desu - It is not fun.)"},{"id":"5034FCF3-9B78-4979-A81A-0B49C8F4DEED","text":"Counting Days in Japanese\n\n• Days 1-10 and the 20th have special readings. Memorize these!\n\n    * 1st: tsuitachi (ついたち)\n    * 2nd: futsuka (ふつか)\n    * 3rd: mikka (みっか)\n    * 4th: yokka (よっか)\n    * 5th: itsuka (いつか)\n    * 6th: muika (むいか)\n    * 7th: nanoka (なのか)\n    * 8th: yooka (ようか)\n    * 9th: kokonoka (ここのか)\n    * 10th: tooka (とおか)\n    * 20th: hatsuka (はつか)\n\n• For days 11-19 and 21-31, combine the number + \"nichi\" (にち).\n\n    * 11th: juuichinichi (じゅういちにち)\n    * 14th: juuyokkanichi is incorrect. Instead use juuyokka (じゅうよっか)\n    * 15th: juugonichi (じゅうごにち)\n    * 23rd: nijuusannichi (にじゅうさんにち)\n    * 31st: sanjuuichinichi (さんじゅういちにち)\n\n• Asking \"What day of the month is it?\"\n\n    *  何日ですか (nan nichi desu ka?)"},{"id":"E249267F-CCC1-4DA2-8C2F-452EFDD68FEF","text":"## Referring to Family Members: Uchi vs. Soto\n\nThe way you refer to family members changes depending on whether you are speaking within your family (uchi\/内) or to someone outside your family (soto\/外). This reflects Japanese modesty and respect. When speaking to someone outside your family, you refer to your own family members with more humble terms.\n\n### Grandparents\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Grandfather: おじいさん (Ojiisan)\n    *   Grandmother: おばあさん (Obaasan)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Grandfather: 祖父 (Sofu)\n    *   Grandmother: 祖母 (Sobo)\n\n### Parents\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Father: お父さん (Otousan)\n    *   Mother: お母さん (Okaasan)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Father: 父 (Chichi)\n    *   Mother: 母 (Haha)\n\n    *Example:* \"My father works at a bank.\"\n    *Speaking to a friend:* 父は銀行で働いています。(Chichi wa ginkou de hataraiteimasu.)\n\n### Siblings\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Older Brother: お兄さん (Oniisan)\n    *   Older Sister: お姉さん (Oneesan)\n    *   Younger Brother: 弟 (Otouto)\n    *   Younger Sister: 妹 (Imouto)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Older Brother: 兄 (Ani)\n    *   Older Sister: 姉 (Ane)\n    *   Younger Brother: 弟 (Otouto)\n    *   Younger Sister: 妹 (Imouto)\n\n    *Example:* \"My older brother is a doctor.\"\n    *Speaking to a colleague:* 兄は医者です。(Ani wa isha desu.)\n\n    *Note:* For younger siblings, the term is the same both inside and outside the family.\n\n### Spouse\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Husband: あなた (Anata), 旦那さん (Danna-san) (less common when directly addressing)\n    *   Wife: あなた (Anata), 奥さん (Okusan) (less common when directly addressing)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Husband: 夫 (Otto), 主人 (Shujin)\n    *   Wife: 妻 (Tsuma), 家内 (Kanai)\n\n    *Example:* \"My wife likes to read books.\"\n    *Speaking to a neighbor:* 妻は本を読むのが好きです。(Tsuma wa hon o yomu no ga suki desu.)\n\n### Children\n\n•   **Uchi (Inside Family):**\n    *   Child: (Child's name) + ちゃん (chan) or くん (kun)\n    *   Son: (Son's name) + くん (kun)\n    *   Daughter: (Daughter's name) + ちゃん (chan)\n•   **Soto (Outside Family):**\n    *   Son: 息子 (Musuko)\n    *   Daughter: 娘 (Musume)\n\n    *Example:* \"My daughter is 5 years old.\"\n    *Speaking to someone at the park:* 娘は5歳です。(Musume wa go-sai desu.)\n\n### Katakana Note\n\nKatakana is generally not used for family member terms except when emphasizing or writing foreign names.\n\n### Grammar Note\n\nWhen talking TO your family members, you always use お父さん etc. When talking ABOUT your family to someone OUTSIDE your family, you use 父 etc.\n\n### Importance\n\nUsing the correct terms shows respect and understanding of Japanese culture. It's an important distinction to make when communicating effectively."},{"id":"73CEFBAA-0E91-48F7-8542-A1B46D1437F9","text":"Particle に and へ for Direction\n\nBoth に and へ can be used to indicate direction or destination when paired with verbs of motion like 行きます (ikimasu - to go), 来ます (kimasu - to come), 帰ります (kaerimasu - to return), etc.\n\nWhen to Use に\n\nUse に when you want to emphasize the specific destination or endpoint of the movement. It highlights the place where the action will be completed.\n\nExamples:\n•   学校に行きます。 (Gakkou ni ikimasu.) - I am going to school. (Focus on school as the destination.)\n•   日本に行きます。 (Nihon ni ikimasu.) - I am going to Japan. (Focus on Japan as the destination.)\n•   家にかえります。 (Ie ni kaerimasu.) - I am returning home. (Focus on home as the destination.)\n\nWhen to Use へ\n\nUse へ when you want to emphasize the direction or the general trajectory of the movement. It suggests moving towards a place rather than arriving at it as the sole focus.\n\nExamples:\n•   学校へ行きます。 (Gakkou e ikimasu.) - I am going towards school. (Focus on the direction of travel.)\n•   駅へ向かいます。 (Eki e mukaimasu.) - I am heading towards the station. (Emphasizes the direction of movement.)\n•   海へ行きたいです。 (Umi e ikitai desu.) - I want to go to the sea. (Implies a general direction.)\n\nInterchangeability\n\nIn many common situations, particularly with 行きます, に and へ are interchangeable and the nuance is very slight.\n\nExample:\n•   東京に行きます。 (Toukyou ni ikimasu.) - I am going to Tokyo.\n•   東京へ行きます。 (Toukyou e ikimasu.) - I am going towards Tokyo.\n\nBoth are perfectly natural and understandable. The choice often depends on personal preference or the specific nuance the speaker wishes to convey."},{"id":"45AD3586-1F3A-4194-8F53-BBF0F24D9196","text":"Grammar Note: 〜てはいけません\n\nThis grammar pattern expresses prohibition, meaning \"must not do\" or \"cannot do.\"\n\n•   Structure: Verb (te-form) + はいけません\n•   Meaning: It is not allowed to do [verb].\n•   Usage: This is a polite but firm way to tell someone they are not permitted to do something.\n\nExample 1:\n食べ物を持ってはいけません。\n(Tabemono o motte wa ikemasen.)\nYou must not bring food.\n\nExample 2:\nここでは話してはいけません。\n(Koko de wa hanashite wa ikemasen.)\nYou must not talk here.\n\nExample 3 (Your sentence):\nまだ家に帰ってはいけません。\n(Mada ie ni kaette wa ikemasen.)\nYou must not go home yet."},{"id":"203208A0-5D77-449C-B7C7-FDF8AEB2C3E3","text":"Adjective + する \n\nGrammar Breakdown: 先生はこの授業を面白くします\n•   **Adjective + する (suru): Making Something [Adjective]**\n    *   The grammar point here is using the i-adjective form plus く (ku) followed by する (shimasu). This construction means \"to make something [adjective].\"\n    *   The base i-adjective is 面白い (omoshiroi) - interesting.\n    *   Changing 面白い to 面白く (omoshiroku) is the adverbial form, which is used before する.\n    *   So, 面白くします (omoshiroku shimasu) means \"makes [it] interesting.\"\n•   **Verb:** します (shimasu) - The polite form of する (suru), meaning \"to do\" or in this case, \"to make.\"\n\nIn summary, the sentence means \"The teacher makes this class interesting.\"\n\nExample:\n•   この本を面白く読みました。(Kono hon o omoshiroku yomimashita.) - I read this book in an interesting way. (Here, 面白く modifies the verb 読みました, meaning \"read interestingly\")\n•   先生はこの本を面白くしました。(Sensei wa kono hon o omoshiroku shimashita.) - The teacher made this book interesting. (Here, 面白く modifies the verb しました, meaning \"made interesting\")"},{"id":"2FC1B400-FECE-4829-B6D6-8E9661AD624F","text":"Grammar Note: Using 「多い」 with Nouns\n\nTopic: Describing the abundance of something.\n\nThe pattern: Noun + が + 多い + Adjective\n\nExplanation:\n•   「多い」 (ooi) is an adjective meaning \"many\" or \"a lot of\".\n•   When describing a noun that has *many* of something, we use the particle 「が」 to link the thing that is numerous to 「多い」.\n•   This construct then functions as an adjective modifying a main noun.\n\nExample:\n•   本が多い部屋 (hon ga ooi heya) - A room with many books.\n•   人が多い場所 (hito ga ooi basho) - A place with many people.\n\nApplying to your sentence:\n•   車が多い道 (kuruma ga ooi michi) - A road with many cars.\n•   Therefore, 車が多い道は危ないです (kuruma ga ooi michi wa abunai desu) means \"A road with many cars is dangerous.\""},{"id":"08418F36-1743-4ED6-AEAC-AAAFE10DD6EE","text":"Understanding Verbs for \"To Work\"\n\nHere's a breakdown of the differences between 働きます, 勤めます, and 仕事します.\n\n働きます (hatarakimasu)\n\n•   This is the most general verb for \"to work\" or \"to be employed.\"\n•   It refers to the act of performing labor or carrying out a job.\n•   It can be used in many contexts, from formal employment to casual labor.\n\nExamples:\n•   会社で働きます。(Kaisha de hatarakimasu.) - I work at a company.\n•   毎日働かなければなりません。(Mainichi hatarakenakereba narimasen.) - I have to work every day.\n•   彼は一生懸命働いている。(Kare wa isshoukenmei hataraite iru.) - He is working hard.\n\n勤めます (tsutomesmasu)\n\n•   This verb specifically means \"to be employed by\" or \"to work for\" a company or organization.\n•   It implies a more formal and stable employment situation.\n•   It focuses on belonging to a specific employer.\n\nExamples:\n•   A社に勤めています。(A-sha ni tsutomete imasu.) - I am employed by Company A.\n•   大学に勤めていた。(Daigaku ni tsutomete ita.) - I was employed by the university.\n•   彼はその会社に長く勤めている。(Kare wa sono kaisha ni nagaku tsutomete iru.) - He has been working for that company for a long time.\n\n仕事します (shigoto shimasu)\n\n•   This is a compound verb meaning \"to do work\" or \"to have a job.\"\n•   It emphasizes the \"work\" (仕事) itself rather than the act of working in a general sense or being employed by someone.\n•   It can refer to a specific task, profession, or the general concept of having something to do.\n\nExamples:\n•   新しい仕事をします。(Atarashii shigoto o shimasu.) - I will do a new job\/task.\n•   私の仕事は医者です。(Watashi no shigoto wa isha desu.) - My job is a doctor. (This uses the noun form, but in speech, one might say 医者の仕事をします - Isha no shigoto o shimasu - I do the work of a doctor.)\n•   今日は忙しくて、たくさんの仕事をしました。(Kyou wa isogashikute, takusan no shigoto o shimashita.) - Today I was busy and did a lot of work."},{"id":"279E97D7-D1A9-4889-9E15-1CC08CE524D0","text":"Question Particle の\n\nThe particle の at the end of a question sentence is a way to soften the question or express mild curiosity. It is primarily used in informal speech and by women and children.\n\n•   Function:\n    *   Makes a question sound more gentle and less direct.\n    *   Often implies the speaker is seeking confirmation or information in a friendly way.\n\n•   Usage:\n    *   Can be added to the end of a declarative sentence to turn it into a question.\n    *   The verb or adjective before の remains in its plain form.\n\n•   Examples:\n    *   これはあなたのカバンです。 (This is your bag.)\n    *   これはあなたのカバンなの？ (Is this your bag? - softer, curious)\n\n    *   元気？ (Are you well? - informal)\n    *   元気なの？ (Are you well? - softer, more curious)\n\n    *   どこへ行くの？ (Where are you going? - informal)\n    *   もう帰るの？ (Are you going home already? - informal)"},{"id":"062B6BFC-955C-47E3-B3C8-9B15159D21E6","text":"Subject Particle vs. Object Particle\n\n•   The particle が (ga) can be used to mark the subject of a verb or adjective, especially when emphasizing it or when it's the first mention of the subject.\n\n•   However, when expressing liking or disliking something, the particle が is often used to mark the object of the liking.\n\n•   In the sentence 私は猫好きで、走るのが大好きです, 猫 (neko) is a noun that describes what the speaker likes. It functions as part of a compound noun or a descriptive phrase meaning \"cat lover\" or \"liking cats.\"\n\n•   When a noun directly follows 好き (suki - like) or 嫌い (kirai - dislike) to indicate what is liked or disliked, you generally do not need a particle like が. The noun is understood to be the object of liking.\n\n    *   Example: 私は犬が好きです。 (Watashi wa inu ga suki desu.) Here, 犬 is the object of liking and is marked by が.\n\n    *   Example: 私は音楽好きです。 (Watashi wa ongaku-zuki desu.) Here, 音楽好き is a compound noun meaning \"music lover.\" 音楽 is not marked by が.\n\n•   In your sentence, 猫好き (neko-zuki) is functioning like the second example above, a compound phrase. You are stating you are a \"cat lover.\"\n\n•   The particle が is used with 走る (hashiru - to run) because 走る is a verb. When a verb is nominalized (turned into a noun phrase) to become the object of liking or disliking, the particle が is used.\n\n    *   Example: 私は本を読むのが好きです。 (Watashi wa hon o yomu no ga suki desu.) Here, 本を読むの (hon o yomu no - the act of reading books) is the nominalized verb phrase, and it is marked by が."}]�_$primaryEdgeSplitViewControllerColumn_&secondaryEdgeSplitViewControllerColumn�O␀9[{"Kanji":"曇り","id":"A2D58000-8304-4E31-AC3C-B049B3010442","English":"Cloudy","example":"","nextDueDate":803252665.964354,"Phonetic":"くもり"},{"Kanji":"建物","id":"B52475C5-FA4B-4AD4-BD67-C086DC9FF95D","English":"Building","example":"","nextDueDate":801351362.465255,"Phonetic":"たてもの"},{"Kanji":"寒い","id":"F4740D2C-96F4-475A-A20E-AC7FF9BE22E3","English":"Cold","example":"","nextDueDate":801351243.9491,"Phonetic":"さむい"},{"Kanji":"雪","id":"DD47B21F-41A7-4D03-B71D-0FCD662F425E","English":"snow","example":"","nextDueDate":801436811.904532,"Phonetic":"ゆき"},{"Kanji":"温泉","id":"81B1972B-E8EA-478A-9E6C-4875B023815B","English":"hot spring","example":"","nextDueDate":807650572.856911,"Phonetic":"おんせん"},{"Kanji":"冬","id":"9AAEEC58-1E9F-4DE2-B23C-906CC4C74086","English":"Winter","example":"","nextDueDate":803239823.645791,"Phonetic":"ふゆ"},{"Kanji":"月","id":"E7E15B06-3C6C-4122-AB20-BBE61F74A513","English":"month","example":"","nextDueDate":801352680.1